+ The output graph is initialized with a single vertex and no edge. Then, for a total of $abs(V) - 1$ times, we add exactly one vertex and one edge to this graph. In the end, it thus has $abs(V)$ vertices, and $abs(V) - 1$ edges. It is also connected, as every edge is formed by connecting an existing vertex to a new one, which is one of its predecessors in $G$. This output is therefore a spanning tree of $G$.

+ This is like Dijkstra, but stopping when all vertices have been found, and writing the output as a tree.
