+ First, building a relation is equivalent to building an adjacency matrix of size $n times n$, so choosing for each of the $n^2$ entries if it will be 0 or 1.
  Reflexivity and (anti)symmetry are independent, since they affect the diagonal and the off-diagonal elements, respectively.
  We can thus treat the three properties separately.
  - If a relation is reflexive, we simply have the diagonal elements fixed to 1, so $n$ fewer entries to set.
  - If it is symmetric, we only need to choose the values in the adjacency matrix for the upper triangle part, and then fill the matrix by symmetry. We therefore have $(n (n-1)) / 2$ fewer binary choices to make.
  - If it is antisymmetric, we can also focus on the upper triangle part, but for each entry $(i,j)$, we have three possibilities:
    - have $a_i cancel(rel) a_j$ and $a_j cancel(rel) a_i$
    - have $a_i rel a_j$ and $a_j cancel(rel) a_i$
    - have $a_i cancel(rel) a_j$ and $a_j rel a_i$

  By the product rule, there are thus $2^((n (n-1)) / 2)$ relations which are both reflexive and symmetric, and $3^((n (n-1)) / 2)$ which are both reflexive and antisymmetric.

+ Let $S_R$ and $S_S$ be the sets of reflexive and symmetric relations.
  What we want to compute is
  $ c = abs(overline(S_R) inter overline(S_S)). $
  By De Morgan, this is equal to $c = abs(overline(S_R union S_S))$.

  Since there are $2^(n^2)$ possible relations in total, $c = 2^(n^2) - abs(S_R union S_S)$.

  Then, by the principle of inclusion-exclusion, we get
  $c = 2^(n^2) - abs(S_R) - abs(S_S) + abs(S_R inter S_S)$.

  From the reasoning given in the previous question, we already know all these terms, so finally:
  $
    c = 2^(n^2) - 2^(n^2 - n) - 2^((n^2 + n) / 2) + 2^((n^2 - n) / 2).
  $

